1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,729
Forty percent to eight. If your benchmark dropped
that far, would you switch models?

2
00:00:05,199 --> 00:00:11,770
That was the story our first chart told. But
before we blamed the model, we followed one
answer through the test.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,485
Here's the setup. On one D G X Spark, we picked a
local model and gave each agent a small job.

4
00:00:20,955 --> 00:00:29,510
Then we changed how many requests we sent, from
five to sixty. We can check every answer against
a known value. Let's follow one request.

5
00:00:30,833 --> 00:00:35,953
Agent eight has a simple job. Find the prime at
index eight, starting from zero.

6
00:00:36,333 --> 00:00:39,960
Count along the row. At index eight, you land on
twenty-three.

7
00:00:40,430 --> 00:00:42,841
Now suppose the agent sends back nineteen.

8
00:00:44,533 --> 00:00:48,565
At a glance, that line looks fine. Right agent
number, right format.

9
00:00:49,633 --> 00:00:53,068
Here's the catch. Nineteen is the prime one slot
earlier.

10
00:00:53,538 --> 00:01:00,450
The format check passes, but the answer check
fails. Feed that exact line to your scorer and
see which check it runs.

11
00:01:01,167 --> 00:01:05,839
Next we sent eight requests together. The server
quietly queued them, one at a time.

12
00:01:06,233 --> 00:01:11,076
With parallel slots turned on, that batch went
from about twenty-seven seconds to eight.

13
00:01:11,302 --> 00:01:19,217
Same number of requests. Very different overlap.
That's why we check what the server actually did
before we interpret the accuracy curve.

14
00:01:19,633 --> 00:01:22,300
Then there were the blank answers. Where did they
go?

15
00:01:22,770 --> 00:01:27,613
The model used up its token budget while
reasoning, and stopped before giving an answer.

16
00:01:28,083 --> 00:01:31,965
We raised the cap from five hundred twelve to two
thousand forty-eight.

17
00:01:32,435 --> 00:01:38,537
On the same task, same prompt, same thirty
agents, correctness went from ten percent to
ninety-three.

18
00:01:39,767 --> 00:01:40,727
One wrinkle.

19
00:01:41,267 --> 00:01:46,643
The bigger groups were getting harder questions.
So we cycled through indices zero to nine.

20
00:01:47,113 --> 00:01:52,894
Now agents eight and eighteen both get index
eight. Smaller groups can still see a different
mix.

21
00:01:55,367 --> 00:01:59,889
Look what happened when we reran it. The steep
drop on that first chart disappeared.

22
00:02:01,033 --> 00:02:07,156
Both Qwen models got every answer right. Deep
Seek stayed at ninety-eight point five percent or
above.

23
00:02:07,626 --> 00:02:10,079
That held through sixty requests on this task.

24
00:02:10,867 --> 00:02:16,179
The remaining misses tell their own story. There
were six, and every one asked for index eight.

25
00:02:16,599 --> 00:02:19,884
Each time Deep Seek said nineteen instead of
twenty-three.

26
00:02:20,304 --> 00:02:29,456
A group of five never gets that item. A group of
sixty sees it six times. You can see why the
individual answers matter as much as the average.

27
00:02:30,067 --> 00:02:33,587
We also tried planting a wrong answer in a leader
message.

28
00:02:34,057 --> 00:02:39,625
None of the four hundred eighty responses
repeated it. That's the result of this particular
test.

29
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:46,324
If your curve falls like ours did, check four
things before you switch models.

30
00:02:46,794 --> 00:02:50,463
Are the requests overlapping? Does the scorer
catch a wrong answer?

31
00:02:50,933 --> 00:02:55,243
Did the model hit its token limit? Are you
comparing the same mix of tasks?

32
00:02:55,713 --> 00:02:58,166
The paper and dashboard walk through all four.
